Google's Chrome for Developers team today <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/23/coalition-of-eu-businesses-challenges-apple/">supported Mozilla's complaint</a> about Apple's rules on non-WebKit browser engines in the European Union, <a href="https://x.com/ChromiumDev/status/2125258930775634095">calling out restrictions</a> that make launching an alternative engine too difficult.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2022/06/Chrome-Feature-22.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-851161" /><br/>
Mozilla recently joined a coalition of up to 55,000 businesses and organizations to ask the European Commission to force Apple to change its browser rules. Apple started allowing alternative browser engines in the EU <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/01/25/third-party-default-browsers-eu-ios-17-4/">in early 2024</a>, but the coalition says no browser maker has been able to transfer its EU users from Apple's WebKit engine to a new engine because of Apple's conditions.<br/>
<br/>
Mozilla explained that using its Gecko browser engine instead of WebKit would require releasing a second Firefox app. Apple says apps with alternative browser engines have to be distributed only in the EU, so Mozilla has been unable to add Gecko to its existing app. Firefox users in the EU would need to transfer to a new app and move all their content, and many users might not be willing to switch. Mozilla said Apple's conditions are "unworkable" and that Apple has only complied with the EU's Digital Markets Act "on paper."<br/>
<br/>
Chrome for Developers echoed the statement in its X post:<br/>
<blockquote>We agree with @mozilla. Nearly 3 years after alternative browser engines on iOS were EU-mandated, zero launched due to remaining Apple restrictions. Forcing browsers to ship separate apps and re-acquire users is choice on paper, not in practice.</blockquote><br/>
Though one Google team expressed support for Mozilla, Google did not sign the open letter to the European Commission. In 2023, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2023/02/04/google-working-on-browser-that-would-break-rules/">Google started work</a> on an experimental version of Chromium for iOS with the Blink engine, but Google said it was <a href="https://www.engadget.com/google-chrome-blink-ios-experiment-220001141.html">only a prototype</a> that would not be released.<br/>
<br/>
In June 2025, Mozilla and Apple <a href="https://open-web-advocacy.org/blog/apples-browser-engine-ban-persists-even-under-the-dma/">participated in a DMA workshop</a> where Mozilla brought up iOS support for third-party browser engines. According to Mozilla, Apple claimed Google and Mozilla have "everything they need" to ship an alternative browser engine in the EU and "have chosen not to do so." In <a href="https://developer.apple.com/support/alternative-browser-engines/">its documentation</a> for alternative browser engines, Apple says its requirements help keep users safe online.<br/>
<br/>
Following the open letter, the European Commission said it <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/24/eu-talks-with-apple-over-rival-iphone-browsers/">is in talks with Apple</a> over its browser engine restrictions. It is also consulting Mozilla and other signatories on how rival browsers can compete with Safari on Apple devices.<div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/chrome/">Chrome</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/google/">Google</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/24/google-ios-eu-browser-rules/">Google Says Apple&#039;s iOS Browser Rules Offer &#039;Choice on Paper, Not in Practice&#039;</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/google-says-apples-ios-browser-rules-offer-choice-on-paper-not-in-practice.2495166/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>